```

Seven AWS services work together, each chosen for a specific reason:

| Service | Role | Why this choice |
|---------|------|-----------------|
| **S3** (Website) | Hosts the static frontend | Private bucket with Origin Access Control — CloudFront-only access |
| **S3** (Attachments) | Temporary file storage | AES-256 encryption, 24-hour auto-delete lifecycle, presigned URL uploads bypass API Gateway's 10 MB limit |
| **CloudFront** | CDN, HTTPS, routing | Single domain for static files and API — no CORS issues. HTTP/2, compression, global edge caching |
| **API Gateway** (HTTP API v2) | Routes `/api/*` to Lambda | Lower latency and cost than REST API. 30-second timeout is fine because Lambda returns immediately |
| **Lambda** | Business logic + web search | 2 GB RAM + 1 GB ephemeral storage handles 100 MB file processing. 5-minute timeout for long model responses. Performs web search via DuckDuckGo/Brave |
| **DynamoDB** | Streaming state | On-demand billing scales to zero. 30-minute TTL auto-cleans records. Stores search results and sources alongside response content |
| **Amazon Bedrock** | AI inference | Managed LLM access — no GPUs to manage. Cross-region routing based on model ID prefix |

## Web search — grounded responses with citations

One of the features I'm most excited about is web search integration. When you enable the **Search** toggle below the textarea, FRIDAY searches the web for relevant information before generating a response. The AI then cites its sources with numbered markers like [1], [2], etc., and you can see exactly where each fact came from.

FRIDAY uses a two-tier search strategy:

- **DuckDuckGo** (default) — No API key needed. Uses the DuckDuckGo HTML lite endpoint, parses the results page, and extracts titles, URLs, and snippets. Works out of the box with zero configuration.
- **Brave Search API** (optional upgrade) — If you provide a Brave API key in the CONFIG settings panel, FRIDAY uses it for richer, more structured results. If Brave fails, it falls through to DuckDuckGo automatically.

The search query is extracted from the user's conversational message by stripping filler words to produce a clean search query. Results are formatted as numbered `[Web Source N]` blocks and injected into the Bedrock prompt alongside a citation instruction. On the frontend, citation markers become clickable links, and a collapsible Sources panel shows each source with its title, domain, snippet, and a "🔍 Searched: [query]" indicator.

## Voice input — talk to FRIDAY

FRIDAY supports voice input through the browser's Web Speech API. Click the **VOICE** button in the input actions bar, and FRIDAY starts listening. Speak your message, and it appears as a live preview above the textarea. When you're done, the transcribed text is inserted into the input field ready to send.

The voice feature uses the `SpeechRecognition` API with continuous recognition and interim results, so you see your words appearing in real time. No external services or API keys needed — all speech recognition happens locally in the browser.

## How streaming works — the fire-and-poll pattern

Here's the core engineering challenge I had to solve: API Gateway has a hard 30-second integration timeout, but a Claude Opus response can take over 2 minutes. You can't stream a long response through API Gateway in a single HTTP connection.

FRIDAY solves this with what I call a "fire-and-poll" pattern:

```
 BROWSER                    LAMBDA                    DYNAMODB         BEDROCK
    │                          │                          │               │
    │  POST /api/chat          │                          │               │
    │─────────────────────────►│                          │               │
    │                          │  PutItem(PENDING)        │               │
    │                          │─────────────────────────►│               │
    │  { conversationId }      │                          │               │
    │◄─────────────────────────│  (kicks off async)       │               │
    │                          │  [web search if enabled] │               │
    │                          │──────────────────────────┼──────────────►│
    │                          │                          │    Stream     │
    │  POST /api/poll (300ms)  │                          │               │
    │─────────────────────────►│  GetItem                 │◄──── chunk ───│
    │  { partial content }     │◄─────────────────────────│               │
    │◄─────────────────────────│  UpdateItem (300ms)      │◄──── chunk ───│
    │         ...              │         ...              │      ...      │
    │                          │                          │               │
    │  POST /api/poll          │  UpdateItem(COMPLETE)    │◄──── done ────│
    │─────────────────────────►│─────────────────────────►│               │
    │  { status: COMPLETE,     │                          │               │
    │    sources, searchQuery }│                          │               │
    │◄─────────────────────────│                          │               │
    │  Stop polling ✓          │                          │               │
```

Here's the step-by-step:

1. The browser sends a chat request to `/api/chat` with the conversation history, model selection, any file attachment metadata, and web search preferences.
2. Lambda creates a `PENDING` record in DynamoDB and returns a `conversationId` — this happens in under 1 second.
3. Lambda then kicks off `processConversation()` asynchronously. If web search is enabled, it first performs the search and formats results as context. Then it calls Bedrock's streaming API and writes accumulated text chunks to DynamoDB every 300 milliseconds.
4. Meanwhile, the browser starts polling `/api/poll` every 300 milliseconds with the `conversationId`.
5. Each poll returns the latest accumulated content, plus any search sources and the search query. The UI renders it incrementally, so it feels like real-time streaming.
6. When Lambda finishes, it marks the DynamoDB record as `COMPLETE` with token usage, search sources, and the search query.
7. The browser detects `COMPLETE`, stops polling, renders citations as clickable links, and displays the sources panel.

This pattern means responses of any length are supported. The UI never freezes. DynamoDB records auto-expire after 30 minutes via TTL.

## File attachments — from browser to Bedrock

FRIDAY supports uploading files up to 100 MB each, with up to 5 files per message — 35+ file types in total. The browser uploads directly to S3 using a presigned PUT URL, bypassing API Gateway's 10 MB payload limit.

| File type | Processing strategy | Dependency |
|-----------|-------------------|------------|
| Text files (TXT, MD, CSV, JSON, XML, YAML, HTML, code, logs) | Read as UTF-8 | None |
| PDF | Text extraction | `pdf-parse` |
| DOCX | Unzip, parse `word/document.xml`, strip XML tags | `adm-zip` |
| XLSX | Unzip, parse shared strings + worksheet XML | `adm-zip` |
| PPTX | Unzip, parse slide XML, strip tags | `adm-zip` |
| Images (JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP) | Base64-encode for Bedrock multimodal | None |

## Smart Summary — reducing token costs by up to 90%

When you enable the "Smart" toggle, FRIDAY pre-processes large files through Amazon Nova Micro ($0.035/1M input tokens) before sending to your chosen model. Files under 2,000 characters are skipped. If summarization fails, the raw text is used instead.

| File | Raw characters | After summary | Reduction |
|------|---------------|---------------|-----------|
| 50K code file | ~50,000 | ~3,000 | 94% |
| 80K PDF report | ~80,000 | ~5,000 | 94% |
| 10K log file | ~10,000 | ~2,000 | 80% |
| 1.5K config file | ~1,500 | ~1,500 (skipped) | 0% |

## Multi-model selection and inference tuning

FRIDAY gives you access to the full range of Amazon Bedrock models from a single dropdown — Claude 4.x, 3.7, 3.5, 3, Amazon Nova, and custom model IDs. Three response style presets (Precise, Balanced, Creative) auto-tune inference parameters, with manual overrides for Temperature, Top P, Top K, and Max Tokens.

When you enable the "Think" toggle for a supported model (Claude 3.7+, 4.x), FRIDAY allocates a 16,000-token thinking budget with visible reasoning chains.

## The cyberpunk UI

The design system is built around a cyberpunk aesthetic — near-black backgrounds, soft royal blue and lavender accents with glow effects, scanline overlays, particle canvas, glitch-text animations, and animated SVG avatars. The frontend is framework-free: vanilla JavaScript, no React, no build step.

Key UI details:
- **Header bar** — Model selector, THEME button, SIZE dropdown, CONFIG button with visible text labels
- **Input area** — Textarea with compact send/stop button beside it; Think/Smart/Search toggles and ATTACH/EXPAND/VOICE buttons below
- **Message actions** — Edit and Copy on user messages; Branch, Retry, and Copy on assistant responses
- **Session cost bar** — Themed gradient design with real-time token counts and USD cost
- **Readable text** — Standard colors (white in dark, dark in light) for message content; cyberpunk accents for UI chrome only
- **Light mode** — Full theme flip via CSS custom property overrides

## Getting started

```bash
cd friday
chmod +x deploy.sh
./deploy.sh
```

The deploy script packages Lambda, deploys CloudFormation, uploads frontend files, and prints your live URL. Open CONFIG to set a system prompt, configure Brave Search API key, or enter a custom model ID.

## What gets created

The single `cloudformation.yaml` provisions: S3 (website + attachments), DynamoDB, Lambda (Node.js 20, 2 GB), API Gateway (4 routes), CloudFront, and IAM roles.

The Lambda backend is five files: `index.js` (routing + streaming + web search orchestration), `webSearch.js` (DuckDuckGo + Brave dual-tier search), `fileProcessor.js` (text extraction + Smart Summary), `promptBuilder.js` (Claude/Nova prompt formatting), `validation.js` (file type/size validation).

## Things to know

**Cost** — AWS infrastructure costs $0–2/month. Bedrock tokens are pay-per-use. Session cost tracker shows real-time spending.

**Web search** — DuckDuckGo works out of the box. Brave Search API key is optional (configure in CONFIG panel, stored in localStorage).

**Voice input** — Uses browser Web Speech API. Works in Chrome, Edge, Safari. No external services needed.

**Security** — Conversations in localStorage only. S3 buckets fully private. Presigned URLs with 15-min expiry. AES-256 encryption. HTTPS enforced.

**Authentication** — Designed for personal use. Add Cognito or WAF for team deployments.

**Error handling** — Graceful degradation throughout: failed PDF extraction → error notice in prompt, failed web search → model responds from training data, failed Smart Summary → raw text used.

